fortable but not fully safe Republican margin.", "watch": "A late surge consolidating anti-incumbent voters behind Hafner, or a strong Democratic environment in Alaska alongside the competitive Senate race, could narrow the margin.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Nick Begich III (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Republican incumbent Nick Begich III faces Democrat Eric Hafner, independent Bill Hill, and a Libertarian in the general election for Alaska's at-large House seat. Begich advanced from the primary with a dominant fundraising operation, having raised about $5.5 million and holding $3.1 million on hand, while Hafner reports no meaningful financial support. Hill has raised over $1.3 million and positions himself alongside Hafner as an anti-incumbent alternative.\n\nEvery major expert rating places the race at Likely R or better, and prediction markets give Democrats only a small chance of an upset. No usable general-election head-to-head polling exists, so the outlook rests on Alaska's Republican lean, Begich's incumbency and money advantage, and the divided opposition field. A Democratic win would require a substantial late shift.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1234, "margin": -7.11}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1042, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0943, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.1159, "margin": -8.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0428, "margin": -11.0}]}